/* ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  DO NOT TYPECHECK WITH THIS FILE.                                        │
   │                                                                          │
   │    tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json     ->  0 errors, exit 0, ALWAYS        │
   │    npm run typecheck                 ->  the real answer                 │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

   This is a SOLUTION file, not a project: `files: []` plus `references` is the
   standard Vite layout, and it means tsc compiles NOTHING when pointed here. It
   does not fail — it succeeds, on any code at all, including code that does not
   compile. There is no compiler flag that makes an empty project loud, which is
   why this warning is a comment.

   It has already caused a wrong conclusion: three separate changes in one
   session were reported as "typecheck clean" on the strength of
   `tsc -p tsconfig.json` exiting 0. Re-run against the real config and the
   project has a 55-error baseline.

   ── Where the real check lives ──────────────────────────────────────────────

     npm run typecheck      tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.app.json   (the truth)
     npm run typecheck:ci   scripts/typecheck-baseline.mjs      (ratchet on 55)
     tsc --build            also correct — it follows `references` — but it
                            writes .tsbuildinfo files and caches incrementally,
                            so a stale cache can under-report. Prefer the
                            scripts above.

   CI runs `npm run typecheck:ci`, so a regression is caught there regardless of
   what anyone types locally. This warning is for the local case, and for the
   next tool wired up to the obvious filename.

   ── Why compilerOptions are here at all ────────────────────────────────────

   Editors and some tooling read the nearest tsconfig.json for path aliases, so
   `baseUrl`/`paths` have to be resolvable from here even though nothing is
   compiled. The loosened flags below mirror tsconfig.app.json so an editor does
   not show a different set of errors than the build does. */
{
  "files": [],
  "references": [
    { "path": "./tsconfig.app.json" },
    { "path": "./tsconfig.node.json" }
  ],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    },
    "noImplicitAny": false,
    "noUnusedParameters": false,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": false,
    "strictNullChecks": false
  }
}
